# Where a heat pump's extra heat comes from
subtitle: "Follow the refrigerant round its loop to see how one kilowatt-hour of electricity carries two more in from cold outdoor air."
tags: ["heat pump","thermodynamics","refrigeration cycle","heating efficiency","renewable energy","coefficient of performance"]
teaches: ["concepts/heat-pump-operation","concepts/energy-transfer","concepts/coefficient-of-performance","methods/refrigerant-loop-analysis"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Puck | an upbeat engineer delighted by a clever machine; relaxed and good-humoured","take":"lines"}

## Where a heat pump's extra heat comes from
vars: {"T":7,"q":2,"Q":3}

    box outdoor "Outdoor coil" #2f6b8f x 250 y 260 font_size 30 hidden true
    box compressor "Compressor" x 250 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    box indoor "Indoor coil" #b5482e x 720 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    box valve "Expansion valve" x 720 y 260 font_size 30 hidden true
    group parts members ["outdoor","compressor","indoor","valve"] hidden true
    path wall #8f887c points [[485,140],[485,680]] style.stroke_width 6 hidden true
    text t_out "outside: {T} °C" #2f6b8f x 250 y 670 font_size 30 hidden true
    text t_inside "inside: 20 °C" #b5482e x 720 y 670 font_size 30 hidden true
    group house members ["wall","t_out","t_inside"] hidden true
    arrow a1 outdoor -> compressor #2f6b8f hidden true
    arrow a2 compressor -> indoor #b5482e hidden true
    arrow a3 indoor -> valve #b5482e hidden true
    arrow a4 valve -> outdoor #2f6b8f hidden true
    group loop members ["a1","a2","a3","a4"] hidden true

    card title "Where the extra heat comes from"

Why can a heat pump deliver more heat than the electricity it uses? It sounds like something for nothing, so follow where the extra comes from.
    draw parts house parallel true

On a mild day, plus seven outside, one kilowatt-hour of electricity puts about three kilowatt-hours of heat into the house.
    arrow elec #3d3833 from.x 30 from.y 560 to.x 150 to.y 560 style.stroke_width 4
    text t_el "1 kWh" x 85 y 605 font_size 30
    arrow heat_out #b5482e from.x 820 from.y 560 to.x 960 to.y 560 style.stroke_width 5
    text t_in "{Q} kWh" #b5482e x 895 y 605 font_size 30

The same refrigerant goes round and round these four parts, and the trick is what happens in each one.
    draw loop

Outside, the refrigerant is colder than the air, so heat flows into it.
    focus outdoor a1

That is where the missing two kilowatt-hours come from: the outdoor air.
    arrow heat_in #2f6b8f from.x 30 from.y 260 to.x 120 to.y 260 style.stroke_width 5
    text t_air "{q} kWh" #2f6b8f x 75 y 305 font_size 30

The compressor squeezes the gas, and squeezing a gas makes it hot, hotter than the room. This is the step the electricity pays for.
    focus compressor a2 elec t_el

Now the refrigerant is the hottest thing in the room, so heat flows out of it, into the air you breathe.
    focus indoor a3 heat_out t_in

The valve lets it expand and turn cold again, ready to go back outside for another load.
    focus valve a4

Round it goes. The electricity never becomes most of the heat; it pays to carry heat that was already outside.
    flow a1 a2 a3 a4

An electric heater turns one kilowatt-hour into exactly one. It has nothing to carry.
    text t_heater "heater: 1 → 1" x 880 y 410 font_size 28

    erase t_heater

In frost there is still heat in the air, but the pump must lift it further, from minus fifteen up to room warmth, so each kilowatt-hour carries less.
    animate T -15 q 1 Q 2 duration 4

At minus fifteen a typical air unit gives about two: still double a heater, but no longer three.
    highlight t_in

Lord Kelvin described such a heat multiplier in 1852, a century before heat pumps warmed ordinary homes.
    portrait portrait_lord_kelvin x 895 y 430 width 130 of "Lord Kelvin"

    erase portrait_lord_kelvin

    quiz "At minus fifteen, where does the second kilowatt-hour come from?"
        right: The outdoor air: minus fifteen is far above absolute zero, and the refrigerant in the coil is colder still, so heat flows in.
        + The frosty outdoor air
        * The compressor's electricity
        * Heat stored on warmer days
---
